[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "27.1"]
[White "26. King's Gambit - 3.others"]
[Black "King's Gambit 3.Be2 #01"]
[Result "*"]

1. e4 e5 2. f4 { The King's Gambit was the most popular opening of the Romantic Era of chess. Nearly all the best players of the 19th century played it with both colours, producing a number of highly interesting and spectacular games. However, since the positional school chess came to the fore, its popularity has been steadily declining. In the recent decade, modern computers dealt additional blow to the King's Gambit's reputation, showing that White's compensation is hardly sufficient and faced with precise Black's play, White will struggle to even equalize. Nevertheless, one will occasionally face a King's Gambit aficionado or an opponent trying to use the opening as a surprise weapon, so it is important to be at least basically prepared for it. } exf4 3. Be2 $2 { If 3.Bc4 is the Bishop Gambit, what should this variation be called? Perhaps a Timid Bishop Gambit ? Unfortunately for fans of bishop moves, it is even worse than 3.Bc4. -KEY- } f5 $1 { This enterprising counter-gambit is essentially the refutation. The idea is to play ...d5 after the capture, retake on f5 and have both the central control and the pawn advantage. } 4. exf5 Qh4+ 5. Kf1 d5 { Now we see the merits of 3...f5! Black takes the centre and regains the pawn. } 6. Nc3 c6 7. d4 Bd6 $19 { Black achieved a winning advantage in Crowl-Purdy, corr 1946 } { _ } 
 *

