1. e4 e5 2. f4 { The King's Gambit was the most popular opening of the Romantic Era of chess. Nearly all the best players of the 19th century played it with both colours, producing a number of highly interesting and spectacular games. However, since the positional school chess came to the fore, its popularity has been steadily declining. In the recent decade, modern computers dealt additional blow to the King's Gambit's reputation, showing that White's compensation is hardly sufficient and faced with precise Black's play, White will struggle to even equalize. Nevertheless, one will occasionally face a King's Gambit aficionado or an opponent trying to use the opening as a surprise weapon, so it is important to be at least basically prepared for it. } exf4 3. Qf3 $6 { This is too provocative. Black will get a lot of tempi attacking the queen. -KEY- } Nc6 4. c3 $2 Qh4+ { Not allowing White to play d4 and consolidate. } 5. g3 fxg3 6. hxg3 Qe7 $19 { In addition to being a pawn up, Black is threatening to undermine White's centre with ...d5. } 
