1. e4 e5 2. f4 { The King's Gambit was the most popular opening of the Romantic Era of chess. Nearly all the best players of the 19th century played it with both colours, producing a number of highly interesting and spectacular games. However, since the positional school chess came to the fore, its popularity has been steadily declining. In the recent decade, modern computers dealt additional blow to the King's Gambit's reputation, showing that White's compensation is hardly sufficient and faced with precise Black's play, White will struggle to even equalize. Nevertheless, one will occasionally face a King's Gambit aficionado or an opponent trying to use the opening as a surprise weapon, so it is important to be at least basically prepared for it. } exf4 3. Nc3 { -KEY- } Qh4+ 4. Ke2 Ne7 $5 ( 4... d5 { has been the most popular move, leading to great complications, from which, however, White can emerge unscathed. This is why I recommend the rare text move, which prepares the ...d5 strike and sets White more serious problems. } ) 
5. Nf3 Qh5 6. d4 g5 7. Kf2 d6 8. h4 g4 9. Ng1 { This position was reached with White two times by a correspondence player Van der Kemp in 2009. He won both games, but Black's play can be easily improved: } Bg7 10. Nge2 Nbc6 11. Nxf4 g3+ { All forced. } 12. Kxg3 Qxd1 13. Nxd1 Nxd4 14. c3 Ne6 $15 { Black's pieces work more harmoniously than White's, and his structure is less vulnerable. These factors guarantee him a slight edge. } 
